.TH WIZ3D-LAUNCH 1 "October 2026" "wiz3d 0.3.1" "Sparky Stereo OS"
.SH NAME
wiz3d-launch \- start a Direct3D 9 Wine game with wiz3D stereo installed in its prefix
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wiz3d-launch
[\fB\-\-enable\-3d\fR | \fB\-\-disable\-3d\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
For a Wine game started with WINEPREFIX set.
Puts wiz3D in the prefix's system folders in front of DXVK (DXVK's d3d9.dll is kept as d3d9_real.dll, the rest are
links into /usr/lib/wiz3d), runs \fIcommand\fR through
.BR wiz3d-run (1),
which declares the game's window to KWin as full side by side, left eye first, and takes wiz3D out again when the game ends.
The prefix and the game's folder come out as they went in.
wiz3D's logs and statistics go to WIZ3D_LOG_DIR, by default ~/.local/state/wiz3d.
Paths handed to the Windows side are named the way Wine names them (a drive of the prefix, or \\\\?\\unix\\...),
so a prefix without the Z: drive works too.
A 32-bit prefix (WINEARCH=win32) gets the x86 tree in its system32; a 64-bit prefix gets x64 in system32 and x86 in syswow64.
.PP
It needs DXVK in the prefix for Direct3D 9 and says so when it is not there.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-3d
Use wiz3D (default).
.TP
.B \-\-disable\-3d
Take wiz3D out of the prefix, if an earlier run left it, and run \fIcommand\fR as it is.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
WINEPREFIX
The prefix to use (required).
.TP
WIZ3D_ENABLE_3D
0 does what \fB\-\-disable\-3d\fR does.
.TP
WIZ3D_LOG_DIR
The folder for wiz3D's logs and Statistic.xml.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The game's exit status.
.SH AUTHOR
Sparky Stereo OS, an edition of SparkyLinux, created by Pawe\(sl "pavroo" Pijanowski and Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack).
